ry, so they apply to every subprocess a sandboxed command starts, such as `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`kubectl`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`terraform`}),`, or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`npm`}),`, not only to Claude’s file tools. Your `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/sandboxing#permission-rules`,children:`permission rules`}),` feed the same lists: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Edit`}),` allow and deny rules join `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`allowWrite`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyWrite`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Read`}),` deny rules join `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyRead`}),`, and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WebFetch`}),` allow and deny rules join the `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#sandbox-network`,children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`network`})}),` domain lists. Every list merges across settings files. When you edit a list during a session, Claude Code `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/settings#when-edits-take-effect`,children:`applies the change to the running session`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`sandbox-path-prefixes`,children:`Sandbox path prefixes`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Paths in `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`allowWrite`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyWrite`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyRead`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`allowRead`}),`, and `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#sandbox-credentials-files`,children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`credentials.files`})}),` resolve by their prefix:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(Table,{children:[_jsx(_components.thead,{children:_jsxs(_components.tr,{children:[_jsx(_components.th,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:`Prefix`}),_jsx(_components.th,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:`Meaning`}),_jsx(_components.th,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:`Example`})]})}),_jsxs(_components.tbody,{children:[_jsxs(_components.tr,{children:[_jsx(_components.td,{\"data-numeric\":`true`,style:{textAlign:`left`},children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/`})}),_jsx(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:`Absolute path from filesystem root`}),_jsxs(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/tmp/build`}),` stays `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/tmp/build`})]})]}),_jsxs(_components.tr,{children:[_jsx(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/`})}),_jsx(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:`Relative to home directory`}),_jsxs(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.kube`}),` becomes `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`$HOME/.kube`})]})]}),_jsxs(_components.tr,{children:[_jsxs(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`./`}),` or no prefix`]}),_jsxs(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:[`Relative to the project root for project settings, or to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.claude`}),` for user settings`]}),_jsxs(_components.td,{style:{textAlign:`left`},children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`./output`}),` in `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`.claude/settings.json`}),` resolves to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`\u003cproject-root\u003e/output`})]})]})]})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`//path`}),` prefix for absolute paths also works. If you use single-slash `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/path`}),` expecting project-relative resolution, switch to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`./path`}),`. This syntax differs from `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/permissions#read-and-edit`,children:`Read and Edit permission rules`}),`, which use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`//path`}),` for absolute and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/path`}),` for project-relative: sandbox filesystem paths use standard conventions, so `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/tmp/build`}),` is an absolute path.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Claude Code strips a trailing slash from a directory path, so `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.aws`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.aws/`}),` match the same directory. Before v2.1.224, Claude Code passed the trailing slash through to the sandbox, and Claude could still read or write paths under a `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyRead`}),` or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyWrite`}),` entry written with one.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Claude Code also removes a trailing `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/**`}),`, so `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/build/**`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/build`}),` cover the same directory. Whether a wildcard such as `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`*`}),` works depends on which list the entry is in and on the platform:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsxs(_components.strong,{children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`allowWrite`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyWrite`})]}),`: on macOS, wildcards work. On Linux and WSL2, the sandbox mounts concrete paths, so Claude Code skips an entry that contains `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`*`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`?`}),`, or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`[`}),` once the trailing `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/**`}),` is removed, and that entry has no effect. Claude Code adds the paths from your `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Edit`}),` permission rules to these lists, so the same limit applies to them, and the `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Config`}),` tab of `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/sandbox`}),` warns about `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Edit`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Read`}),` permission rules that contain wildcards.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsxs(_components.strong,{children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyRead`}),` and `,_jsx(_component