oot\u003e/output`})]})]})]})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`//path`}),` prefix for absolute paths also works. If you use single-slash `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/path`}),` expecting project-relative resolution, switch to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`./path`}),`. This syntax differs from `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/permissions#read-and-edit`,children:`Read and Edit permission rules`}),`, which use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`//path`}),` for absolute and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/path`}),` for project-relative: sandbox filesystem paths use standard conventions, so `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/tmp/build`}),` is an absolute path.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Claude Code strips a trailing slash from a directory path, so `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.aws`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.aws/`}),` match the same directory. Before v2.1.224, Claude Code passed the trailing slash through to the sandbox, and Claude could still read or write paths under a `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyRead`}),` or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyWrite`}),` entry written with one.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Claude Code also removes a trailing `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/**`}),`, so `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/build/**`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/build`}),` cover the same directory. Whether a wildcard such as `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`*`}),` works depends on which list the entry is in and on the platform:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsxs(_components.strong,{children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`allowWrite`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyWrite`})]}),`: on macOS, wildcards work. On Linux and WSL2, the sandbox mounts concrete paths, so Claude Code skips an entry that contains `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`*`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`?`}),`, or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`[`}),` once the trailing `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/**`}),` is removed, and that entry has no effect. Claude Code adds the paths from your `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Edit`}),` permission rules to these lists, so the same limit applies to them, and the `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Config`}),` tab of `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`/sandbox`}),` warns about `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Edit`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Read`}),` permission rules that contain wildcards.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsxs(_components.strong,{children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`denyRead`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`allowRead`})]}),`: wildcards work on every platform. On Linux and WSL2, Claude Code expands a read entry to the concrete paths it matches, which it doesn’t do for the write lists.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`sandbox-filesystem-allowwrite`,children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`sandbox.filesystem.allowWrite`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Add paths where sandboxed commands can write, beyond the working directory and the session temp directory. Use it when a subprocess such as `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`kubectl`}),` or a build tool needs to write outside the project.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Scope`}),`: `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#scopes`,children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Any file`})})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Type`}),`: array of path strings, using the `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#sandbox-path-prefixes`,children:`sandbox path prefixes`})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Default`}),`: unset, so sandboxed commands can write only to the working directory and the session temp directory`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_comp